---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';

/**
 * One premise: AI makes production cheaper, so design effort shifts toward
 * defining the problem, constraining the output, comparing alternatives, and
 * reserving consequential decisions for people. Four sections, one per thing.
 *
 * The brief, the six criteria and the three limits are written out as headed
 * sections rather than ledger rows. A ledger is right when a row is a label and
 * a line; each of these carries a paragraph explaining why it is on the list,
 * and that does not fit in a cell.
 */
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="I define the conditions in which AI produces design work"
  metaTitle="I define the conditions in which AI produces design work | Tyler Young"
  description="An agent can produce a polished interface in minutes. That speed does not tell the team whether the interface solves the right problem. The brief, system, review loop and perimeter I build for agents to work inside."
  eyebrow="AI-assisted product design"
  current="building"
  meta={[
    ['Practice', 'AI-assisted product design'],
    ['My role', 'Frame · constrain · evaluate · decide'],
    ['Output', 'Working options · production-ready systems · clearer decisions'],
    ['Principle', 'AI expands the available options. People remain responsible for what those options mean and what ships.'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    An agent can produce a polished interface in minutes. That speed does not
    tell the team whether the interface solves the right problem.
  </p>
  <p>
    Someone still has to define the intended outcome, specify the behavior,
    protect the product’s system, compare the options, and decide what is good
    enough to ship. Those are design decisions, and I remain accountable for
    them.
  </p>
  <p>
    AI changes how quickly those decisions become working software. A process
    that once produced one direction in several weeks can now produce several
    working directions in a few hours. The team can compare actual behavior
    instead of predicting how static screens might behave.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because implementation happens earlier, my decisions must also happen
    earlier. A rule cannot wait for a handoff document if the agent is already
    building. It must be part of the environment the agent works within.
  </p>
  <p>I build that environment from four things:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>a brief that defines the problem</li>
    <li>a system that constrains the output</li>
    <li>a review loop that compares the options</li>
    <li>a perimeter that reserves certain decisions for people</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The brief defines what the work must accomplish</h2>
  <p>
    An agent can produce a convincing answer to a poorly framed problem. The
    quality of the output therefore depends first on the quality of the brief.
  </p>
  <p>Before generating an interface, I answer six questions.</p>

  <h3>Intent</h3>
  <p>Who is trying to do what, and which decision should become easier?</p>
  <p>
    This establishes the purpose of the work. Without it, the agent can optimize
    the screen without improving the user’s situation.
  </p>

  <h3>Required behavior</h3>
  <p>
    What must remain true across every state, transition, permission, and
    failure path?
  </p>
  <p>
    A default screen shows only one moment. The behavioral requirements define
    the product across the moments the screen does not show.
  </p>

  <h3>System constraints</h3>
  <p>Which tokens, components, and patterns must the work use?</p>
  <p>
    These constraints connect the new interface to the existing product. They
    prevent each generated option from creating its own visual and behavioral
    rules.
  </p>

  <h3>Prohibited actions</h3>
  <p>Which actions require a person?</p>
  <p>
    Some decisions affect access, safety, money, or other people. The brief
    identifies where the product may assist and where it must stop for approval.
  </p>

  <h3>Open questions</h3>
  <p>What should the work help the team learn?</p>
  <p>
    Not every part of the problem is settled before exploration begins. Naming
    the uncertainty turns generated options into a way to investigate it.
  </p>

  <h3>Evaluation criteria</h3>
  <p>What would make the result wrong even if it looked complete?</p>
  <p>These criteria prevent polish from becoming evidence of quality.</p>

  <p>
    Together, the six answers describe more than a screen. They define the
    purpose, behavior, constraints, risks, unknowns, and standard by which the
    result will be judged.
  </p>

  <h2>The design system constrains what AI can produce</h2>
  <p>
    A brief defines the problem. The design system defines the approved language
    for solving it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every colour, type style, spacing value, radius, component, interaction
    pattern, and accessibility rule resolves to the system. If the required
    value or pattern does not exist, the agent reports the gap instead of
    inventing a local solution.
  </p>
  <p>
    This changes the role of the system. It is no longer a reference that
    someone checks after implementation. It becomes part of the environment from
    which the implementation is generated.
  </p>
  <p>
    At Atheer, I rebuilt an aging design system into this kind of production
    pipeline. Exploration returned as working components. Designers could test
    new directions without breaking the product’s coherence. Engineers received
    behavior in code. Brand and accessibility rules applied before review rather
    than being corrected afterward.
  </p>
  <p>
    The product and its component library are both running. The library
    documents the states and variations that connect the system to the product.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a class="link" href="/ops-manager">See the operations product and its design system</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    When the system directly governs production, it does more than keep the
    interface consistent. It defines which changes the product can accept
    without creating a new rule.
  </p>

  <h2>The review loop turns options into decisions</h2>
  <p>
    The brief and system can produce several credible options. They cannot
    decide which option best resolves the problem.
  </p>
  <p>
    Generating three versions may now be cheap. Comparing their behavior,
    consequences, and tradeoffs still requires judgment.
  </p>
  <p>
    On a recent engagement, I built three approaches to the same problem and
    published each at a separate URL. The team could use the alternatives, read
    their real content, and encounter their edge cases. That gave us stronger
    evidence than three static pictures could provide.
  </p>
  <p>
    Running software also exposes disagreements that specifications can hide. A
    designer and engineer may use the same term but expect different behavior.
    An agent may satisfy a sentence while missing the reason behind it. Once
    those interpretations become working software, the team can see and discuss
    the difference.
  </p>
  <p>I compare every option against six criteria.</p>

  <h3>Intent</h3>
  <p>Does it solve the problem the team agreed to solve?</p>

  <h3>Behavior</h3>
  <p>Do its states, transitions, permissions, and failure paths make sense?</p>

  <h3>System</h3>
  <p>Does it strengthen the product’s existing patterns or weaken them?</p>

  <h3>Content</h3>
  <p>
    Does the interface explain what happened and what the user should do next?
  </p>

  <h3>Accessibility</h3>
  <p>Can people perceive, understand, and operate it under real conditions?</p>

  <h3>Implementation</h3>
  <p>Can the proposed behavior work credibly in the actual product?</p>

  <p>
    An interface can be clean and consistent while failing one of these tests.
    That is why visual polish is not the end of the review.
  </p>
  <p>
    I do not need to draw every artifact by hand to maintain a standard. I need
    to distinguish work that is coherent from work that is merely plausible,
    explain the difference, and improve the system when the same weakness
    appears repeatedly.
  </p>
  <p>
    A stakeholder review on this site shows that process in practice: two
    running builds, 48 notes, and three examples in which the written request
    and the right product decision were not the same.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a class="link" href="/review-loop">See the stakeholder review loop</a>
  </p>

  <h2>The perimeter defines what AI may decide</h2>
  <p>
    The brief directs the work, the system constrains it, and the review loop
    evaluates it. The perimeter determines which decisions the agent may make
    along the way.
  </p>
  <p>An agent can:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>generate options within approved patterns</li>
    <li>implement an approved direction</li>
    <li>run defined checks</li>
    <li>identify a missing pattern</li>
    <li>propose an addition to the system</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    It cannot make every unresolved choice by default. I set three limits.
  </p>

  <h3>Do not invent design-system values</h3>
  <p>
    If the system has no approved value or pattern, the agent identifies the
    gap. It does not hide the missing decision inside a component-specific
    solution.
  </p>

  <h3>Do not resolve product ambiguity</h3>
  <p>
    When the requirements support several interpretations, the agent returns the
    question to the team. Choosing one interpretation at speed would still be an
    assumption, even if the result looked finished.
  </p>

  <h3>Do not confuse valid with approved</h3>
  <p>
    A result can compile, pass its checks, and follow the design system while
    still being the wrong thing to build. Technical validity establishes that
    the implementation works as specified. Human approval establishes that the
    specification was worth implementing.
  </p>

  <p>
    These limits matter because faster production also makes misunderstandings
    faster to produce. Before work ships, a person must know what changed, which
    checks ran, what assumptions remain, and what else the change could affect.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Alucify, I designed the product surfaces that make those distinctions
    visible.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a class="link" href="/alucify">See the Alucify case study</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    I automate execution more readily than judgment because execution follows an
    established decision. Judgment determines which decision the product should
    embody.
  </p>

  <h2>AI changes the material of design work</h2>
  <p>
    AI does not remove the need for design. It changes where design decisions
    have the most leverage.
  </p>
  <p>
    When producing each screen was expensive, much of the work centered on
    creating and handing off individual artifacts. When working software becomes
    cheap to generate, the larger constraint is the quality of the environment
    producing it.
  </p>
  <p>That environment includes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>the brief that defines the outcome</li>
    <li>the system that limits the available solutions</li>
    <li>the loop that evaluates working options</li>
    <li>the perimeter that assigns responsibility</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Building these conditions allows more of the original decision to survive
    implementation. The designer, engineer, and agent work from the same
    constraints, so fewer assumptions have to cross a handoff through prose
    alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tools are new. The standard is not. The product must still be useful,
    coherent, accessible, explainable, and right for the people it serves.
  </p>
  <p>
    AI increases the amount of work a team can produce. My leverage comes from
    improving the system that determines what kind of work gets produced.
  </p>
</DocumentLayout>
